cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use deduxit in your work, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: deduxit
abstract: >-
  A collection of logic suites: a Rust library and a command line
  program that decide, check, prove step by step and export sequents of
  linear logic, classical and intuitionistic, of classical,
  intuitionistic and minimal propositional logic, and of propositional
  modal logic in the fifteen systems from K to S5. Proofs are shown as
  derivation trees, proof nets or tableaux, and classical sequents also
  by their truth tables; a sequent that is not valid gets a Kripke
  countermodel, and a modal one that is valid a derivation in a
  labelled sequent calculus. It checks and builds derivations in calculi given as
  rule files, natural deduction in Gentzen's and Fitch's styles among
  them. Its code, tests and documentation were written with
  substantial AI assistance, by Anthropic's Claude models in Claude
  Code, in steps the author planned, directed and accepted, and the
  author is responsible for them. Its results do not rest on the model
  that wrote it. An independent checker validates every proof and every
  countermodel it returns. Classical proofs export as certificates that
  the Rocq proof assistant checks, and proofs of every mode and
  countermodels as certificates against deduxit's own Rocq library,
  rocq-deduxit (https://github.com/deduxit/rocq-deduxit). Mutation
  testing and fuzzing measure the tests, and its verdicts on published
  problem libraries are compared with their known status and with those
  of eight other provers.
